<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhelyu Zhelev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1935">1935</a>–<a href="/years/2015">2015</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhelyu Zhelev was a Bulgarian philosopher and dissident whose book on fascism was banned under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. He led the opposition Union of Democratic Forces in 1989 and 1990, was elected president by parliament in 1990 and became the first directly elected president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1992, serving until 1997.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhelyu-zhelev.jpg" alt="Zhelyu Zhelev" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%96%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8E_%D0%96%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Zhelev was born on 3 March 1935 into a modest village family in Veselinovo in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. He studied philosophy at Sofia University, graduating in 1958 and gaining a PhD in 1974. A member of the Bulgarian Communist Party, he was expelled from it for political reasons in 1965. All employment in Bulgaria was state-regulated, so he was unemployed for six years, a condition termed social parasitism in communist terminology. He spent much of that time in virtual internal exile in his wife's village, scraping a living from odd jobs on farms.</p><p>In 1982 Zhelev published his book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a>. Three weeks after publication it was removed from bookstores and libraries throughout the country, because its description of the fascist states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> made these regimes comparable to the Communist regimes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a>. In 1988 he founded the Ruse Committee, and in 1989 he became a founding member and chairman of the Club for Support of Openness and the Reform. This helped him to the chairmanship of the Coordinating Council of the Union of Democratic Forces. He was one of the most prominent figures of the 1989 Bulgarian Revolution, which ended the 35-year rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todor-zhivkov">Todor Zhivkov</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Zhelev was elected a member of parliament in June 1990 to the 7th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-national-assembly-of-turkey">Grand National Assembly</a>, whose main task was to draft a new democratic constitution. After President Petar Mladenov resigned, the Assembly elected Zhelev his successor on 1 August 1990, the first head of state in 44 years who was neither a Communist nor a fellow traveller. The constitution of July 1991 provided for direct election of the president, for at most two terms, and made the office largely representative.</p><p>In the first such election, in January 1992, he led the first round on 12 January and won the runoff a week later against Velko Valkanov, backed by the Socialists, with 52.8% of the votes. He suspended his membership in the Union of Democratic Forces, believing the presidency should be above politics. In 1996 he lost the party's nomination for a second term to Petar Stoyanov.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his presidency ended in 1997 Zhelev became honorary chair of the Liberal Democratic Union and of Liberal International and set up a foundation named after him. He also founded and presided over the Balkan Political Club, a union of former political leaders from Southeast Europe. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> on 30 January 2015, aged 79.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhelyu Zhelev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The philosopher <b>Zhelyu Zhelev</b> became the first directly elected president.</blockquote><a href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Veselinovo, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.98,27.03&amp;year=1987" aria-label="Veselinovo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5203 637 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhelyu Zhelev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5653 907h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5653 907h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.98,27.03&amp;year=1987">What was here in 1987? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
